WEBVTT

01:07.250 --> 01:08.958
I took her hearing offline.

01:09.042 --> 01:11.083
[Kirsh] She mentioned a sound
the moment we landed.

01:11.875 --> 01:14.042
It was louder the closer
to the Xenomorph she got.

01:15.667 --> 01:18.375
Then last night in the lab
when I extracted the embryo…

01:18.458 --> 01:20.792
You think it's got something
to do with these creatures?

01:20.875 --> 01:23.417
Not all of them. Just one species.

01:23.500 --> 01:24.500
[Dame Sylvia] Hmm.

01:24.583 --> 01:29.458
[vocalizing] Ma. Ah. Ah. Ah.

01:29.542 --> 01:31.083
[sighs] Stop frowning. It's not a wake.

01:31.167 --> 01:34.333
We're on a voyage of discovery,
and I have a colonic in 30 minutes.

01:34.417 --> 01:37.750
Go, uh, push some buttons and turn some
knobs while the grown-ups talk.

01:39.875 --> 01:42.583
We've taken her offline.
We think she can hear--

01:42.667 --> 01:44.917
The Xenomorph, yeah. Just assume
I'm ahead of you always.

01:45.000 --> 01:48.917
Which… Don't you have little, you know,
little experiments to run?

01:49.000 --> 01:51.958
These creatures
aren't gonna discover themselves.

01:54.750 --> 01:55.750
Of course.

01:58.125 --> 01:59.125
You too.

02:02.167 --> 02:04.833
No. She's scared. She needs me.

02:04.917 --> 02:07.583
Ah, careful. You're not her mother.

02:07.667 --> 02:09.833
It's an IT issue, not a gab session.

02:09.917 --> 02:13.667
And I'm still waiting on your field
assessments of the hybrid performance.

02:17.583 --> 02:20.292
I'll be back. Okay?

02:27.375 --> 02:29.500
[sighs] Alone at last.

02:30.000 --> 02:31.000
Okay.

02:31.083 --> 02:32.625
Let's bring her hearing online…

02:32.708 --> 02:34.000
[beeping rapidly, stops]

02:34.083 --> 02:36.292
…figure out
if it's a hardware or a software issue.

02:38.750 --> 02:41.708
-Oh, good. It's the brother.
-Are you okay?

02:41.792 --> 02:43.167
-Uh, she can't hear you.
-[exhales sharply]

02:43.250 --> 02:45.750
-Why?
-Uh, we took her hearing offline.

02:45.833 --> 02:47.708
-He did what?
-Hi.

02:49.167 --> 02:51.583
We haven't met. You, uh…

02:51.667 --> 02:55.458
You work f-for me.
Or should I say you work for a, uh,

02:55.542 --> 02:58.875
subsidiary of a subsidiary of
a holding company of an LLC of mine.

02:58.958 --> 03:00.250
Yeah, how's the new lung?

03:01.292 --> 03:03.167
[stammers] It's great. Thanks.

03:03.250 --> 03:05.583
You'll never guess
what we did with the old one.

03:06.500 --> 03:09.125
-What?
-I can't hear you.

03:10.625 --> 03:12.250
I can't hear.

03:12.333 --> 03:16.208
There was a-a sound in the lab.
It hurt my head. I-I can't hear.

03:16.292 --> 03:18.000
Okay. [chuckles]

03:18.083 --> 03:19.875
What's in the lab?

03:21.083 --> 03:22.750
[smacks lips]

03:27.917 --> 03:30.042
Tell me you didn't bring
those things back here.

03:33.625 --> 03:36.750
We're wasting time. Okay, bring
her audio processors online.

03:37.708 --> 03:39.750
Yeah. [clears throat] Okay.

03:45.125 --> 03:49.958
[sighs] All right. Can you hear my voice?

03:52.000 --> 03:56.292
[muffled] Can you hear my voice?
Can you hear my voice?

03:57.167 --> 04:01.708
-Yeah. Yeah. [chuckles]
-Yeah? Can you hear the sound?

04:02.958 --> 04:06.833
-No.
-[Arthur] No? Okay, how's this?

04:10.167 --> 04:12.917
-Okay. Okay.
-Okay?

04:13.000 --> 04:15.458
-Okay. [laughing]
-Okay.

04:15.542 --> 04:18.167
Hey. Hey, Biscuit.

04:18.667 --> 04:19.750
Hi.

04:20.583 --> 04:21.833
Where were you?

04:22.917 --> 04:27.542
I… I had a surgery,
but it's okay. I'm fine.

04:27.625 --> 04:29.042
-[Arthur] Wendy.
-I'm all better.

04:29.125 --> 04:30.708
-[Arthur] Wendy.
-Hmm?

04:31.833 --> 04:33.500
Can you tell me what happened?

04:37.708 --> 04:41.917
I woke up and I heard it. The sound like…

04:43.000 --> 04:46.417
[chirring]

04:47.917 --> 04:50.208
Like bugs rubbing their legs together.

04:51.125 --> 04:52.958
-Crickets?
-Hmm.

04:53.750 --> 04:57.750
So I followed it. And it was coming
from the lab.

04:59.125 --> 05:00.125
Kirsh was…

05:02.250 --> 05:07.417
Kirsh was doing things in there.
He was experimenting on the eggs.

05:08.000 --> 05:10.042
-[tearing]
-[chirring]

05:13.042 --> 05:14.125
It was screaming.

05:16.083 --> 05:17.208
[Hermit] The eggs?

05:17.292 --> 05:18.292
No.

05:20.875 --> 05:21.958
The baby.

05:25.500 --> 05:28.667
-The baby was screaming.
-[squealing]

05:35.833 --> 05:39.958
Okay, we're gonna figure out what it is
you're hearing and why you're hearing it,

05:40.042 --> 05:41.292
and we're gonna make it stop.

05:41.833 --> 05:43.292
-No.
-No.

05:43.375 --> 05:44.375
[scoffs]

05:44.458 --> 05:46.375
-What-What do you mean no?
-She… [scoffs]

05:46.458 --> 05:48.458
…She can hear an alien language
and you wanna turn that off?

05:48.542 --> 05:51.042
If it's hurting you,
we should make it stop.

05:51.125 --> 05:53.333
No, I-I think they picked me.

05:55.792 --> 06:00.083
-They're talking to me. I wanna hear them.
-Deal. Start the test.

06:01.917 --> 06:04.125
[sighs] Okay.

06:06.417 --> 06:09.042
Let's start the test at 20,000 Hz.

06:16.375 --> 06:17.375
[chirring]

06:17.458 --> 06:18.625
I hear it.

06:20.125 --> 06:22.333
Okay, that's on low. What if I raise it?

06:25.708 --> 06:26.833
Same.

06:26.917 --> 06:29.458
Must be spread
across multiple frequencies.

06:29.542 --> 06:32.333
Uh, I'm gonna go to the highest range now.

06:35.292 --> 06:37.292
-[chirring sound escalating]
-[ticking]

06:42.417 --> 06:45.333
-Stop, stop, stop, stop. Down.
-Down.

06:46.375 --> 06:47.458
There.

06:48.833 --> 06:50.500
[snaps fingers]
Curly, did you hear anything?

06:52.250 --> 06:54.042
-Like what?
-A sound you can't identify.

06:55.583 --> 06:56.583
Maybe.

06:56.667 --> 06:58.958
Don't tell me what I wanna hear.
Tell me the truth.

07:04.250 --> 07:05.250
Move.

07:08.250 --> 07:11.792
Can you make the sound
in a frequency we can hear?

07:12.417 --> 07:13.417
How?

07:14.750 --> 07:17.458
How did you hack the data
to watch your brother? Hmm?

07:17.958 --> 07:19.333
Asimov said above a certain level

07:19.417 --> 07:22.000
technology
is indistinguishable from magic.

07:23.083 --> 07:26.792
Don't think of your voice as a voice.
Think of it as a speaker,

07:26.875 --> 07:28.375
just plays music.

07:29.292 --> 07:34.458
The sound is in your mainframe,
so you just… [blows] …Alakazam.

07:48.750 --> 07:49.750
[whirs]

07:52.042 --> 07:54.042
[chirring]

07:58.208 --> 08:01.833
[chirring continues]

08:28.667 --> 08:30.000
[taps, exhales]

08:30.083 --> 08:32.250
Sure you speak French,
but can you do that?

08:35.542 --> 08:36.958
-Enough.
-[chirring continues]

08:37.042 --> 08:39.833
-Oh, the brother's having a feeling.
-She needs to rest.

08:39.917 --> 08:41.958
She has a lithium-ion battery
that could power a small city.

08:42.042 --> 08:43.250
She doesn't need to rest. [chuckles]

08:43.333 --> 08:46.083
She's not a machine.
She is a child and she's frightened.

08:46.167 --> 08:47.250
I can speak for myself.

08:49.083 --> 08:52.125
She-She needs a break, sir.
[stammers] It's been a rough night.

08:55.625 --> 08:56.625
Of course.

08:57.833 --> 08:59.292
Just take all the time you need.

09:04.042 --> 09:05.333
Wanna go show me your room?

09:08.125 --> 09:09.125
Yeah.

09:30.125 --> 09:31.583
[exhales deeply]

09:34.875 --> 09:35.875
What do you think?

09:37.958 --> 09:44.500
I think when a hostile alien species
decides to speak, one needs to ask why.

09:44.583 --> 09:47.708
Yeah, unless it's not her they're talking
to, it's each other and she can hear them.

09:47.792 --> 09:50.083
-And now they know that too.
-[chuckles]

09:51.917 --> 09:54.292
Are you sure you wouldn't like
to return them to Yutani

09:54.375 --> 09:55.917
and get back to the work at hand?

09:56.000 --> 09:59.375
So I can wake up one day with
a Xenomorph in my belly? No, thank you.

10:01.833 --> 10:04.500
Tell the brother he can stay,
but he's working for us.

10:05.458 --> 10:06.625
-The conflict.
-No.

10:06.708 --> 10:10.958
Just a reminder.
It's my world. He just lives in it.

10:14.458 --> 10:15.458
[rain falling]

10:22.250 --> 10:25.375
I've been drawing a lot.

10:31.333 --> 10:33.917
And, uh… and this is my bed.

10:36.333 --> 10:39.917
Well, it's more like a charging station,
but it… it's kind of like a bed.

10:40.000 --> 10:41.292
[chuckles]

10:42.958 --> 10:43.958
You don't sleep?

10:44.875 --> 10:49.042
Oh, I… [stammers]
No, not really. [chuckles]

11:04.375 --> 11:05.417
Are you mad at me?

11:08.167 --> 11:11.208
[chuckling] What?
Why would I be mad at you?

11:11.292 --> 11:13.833
Because I wanted to hear the aliens. I…

11:18.958 --> 11:22.125
Because you had
to go to a cemetery. For me.

11:24.458 --> 11:25.458
No.

11:27.083 --> 11:28.083
Come on.

11:30.500 --> 11:31.667
I'm not mad at you.

11:33.583 --> 11:34.917
I'm mad at them.

11:37.333 --> 11:38.333
Why?

11:39.250 --> 11:42.208
Because they wouldn't let me see you
when you were in the hospital.

11:43.667 --> 11:44.750
Or Dad.

11:50.042 --> 11:51.125
So you wanted to come?

11:53.208 --> 11:55.917
Of course I wanted to come.

11:58.292 --> 11:59.833
You just… You couldn't find a way?

12:00.875 --> 12:04.667
If there was a way, I would have found it.

12:05.875 --> 12:11.833
But there was a war,
and I was on the other side of the earth.

12:17.917 --> 12:18.958
Okay.

12:22.500 --> 12:24.333
I'm here now though.

12:28.500 --> 12:29.542
[chuckles]

12:42.750 --> 12:44.917
[Boy Kavalier] "The Neverland had been
make-believe in those days,

12:45.000 --> 12:46.333
but it was real now…

12:48.542 --> 12:49.833
and there were no night-lights,

12:51.250 --> 12:53.750
and it was getting darker every moment."

12:59.708 --> 13:01.917
-[slightly sobbing]
-[Morrow] When is a machine not a machine?

13:02.000 --> 13:03.000
[Slightly] Get off me.

13:03.083 --> 13:05.583
[Morrow] When is a machine not a machine?

13:09.167 --> 13:14.417
"Of all delectable islands
the Neverland is the snuggest

13:14.500 --> 13:16.375
and most compact…

13:16.458 --> 13:18.375
[birds chirping]

13:18.458 --> 13:20.000
[Kavalier] …not large and sprawly, you know,

13:20.083 --> 13:22.042
with tedious distances between

13:22.125 --> 13:25.333
one adventure and another,
but nicely crammed.

13:27.458 --> 13:30.333
When you play at it by day
with the chairs and tablecloth,

13:30.417 --> 13:32.500
it is not in the least alarming,

13:33.208 --> 13:39.083
but in the two minutes before you
go to sleep it becomes very nearly real.

13:40.500 --> 13:42.958
That is why there are night-lights."

13:44.417 --> 13:45.708
[Slightly] We're not friends.

13:46.292 --> 13:49.125
[Morrow] No, I suppose not.
But I want to be.

13:49.208 --> 13:52.958
[Slightly] Friends don't lie.
You said you were Prodigy.

13:53.708 --> 13:55.958
Well, I didn't know
if you were a villain or a hero.

13:59.000 --> 14:00.000
Hero.

14:01.333 --> 14:02.417
Me too.

14:03.250 --> 14:06.125
And since we're both heroes,
I need your help.

14:07.042 --> 14:10.708
Get something and bring it
to me, okay? An egg.

14:10.792 --> 14:12.667
You don't have eggs where you are?

14:13.625 --> 14:14.750
It's not that kind of egg.

14:18.583 --> 14:19.583
A monster egg.

14:20.458 --> 14:22.083
Such a smart boy.

14:23.667 --> 14:25.083
[Slightly] No, I can't. I can't.

14:25.583 --> 14:28.083
[whispers, stammers]
That would be stealing.

14:28.167 --> 14:31.625
But wait. How can you steal from a thief?

14:32.875 --> 14:34.250
[Slightly] Is this another riddle?

14:34.333 --> 14:37.917
A moral inquiry. Do you know
the difference between right and wrong?

14:40.333 --> 14:41.333
[Slightly] I think so.

14:41.417 --> 14:45.000
Well, if that egg belongs to me
and somebody takes it,

14:45.500 --> 14:47.750
how can it be wrong to take it back?

14:49.208 --> 14:55.542
[Slightly] They fixed me here, you know?
Like, Boy Kavalier. This… This is my home.

14:55.625 --> 14:56.875
[sighs]

14:56.958 --> 15:02.708
Now you're talking about loyalty.
That's a very different principle.

15:03.792 --> 15:07.625
People do all kinds of terrible things
in the name of loyalty. No.

15:08.500 --> 15:13.125
If you want to do the moral thing,
you'll help me get back what you stole.

15:14.667 --> 15:17.208
Think of it as restitution.

15:18.500 --> 15:20.958
What's "recstitution"?

15:21.042 --> 15:23.875
It means making things right.

15:25.333 --> 15:26.875
You want to make things right, don't you?

15:26.958 --> 15:29.042
Can I talk to someone about it?
Like Smee or someone?

15:29.125 --> 15:31.083
He always knows the answers to things.

15:31.167 --> 15:33.333
[Morrow] No, you can't talk to anyone.

15:33.417 --> 15:36.833
That would hurt me
and what do we know about hurting people?

15:39.417 --> 15:40.417
It's wrong?

15:41.000 --> 15:42.708
Yes, very good.

15:44.792 --> 15:48.750
Can I trust you? We need to trust each
other if we're gonna make this work.

15:50.625 --> 15:52.708
So I'm going to tell you my name.

15:53.917 --> 15:57.750
It's Kumi. Kumi Morrow.

15:58.375 --> 16:01.250
[chuckles] Kumi? It's a nice name.

16:03.000 --> 16:04.000
It's Ghanaian.

16:06.333 --> 16:07.417
Remind me of your name again?

16:09.667 --> 16:10.667
Slightly.

16:11.250 --> 16:14.500
-No, silly. Your real name.
-Oh.

16:16.417 --> 16:18.208
Not… Not supposed to say.

16:19.500 --> 16:21.958
Well, how can I trust you
if you won't tell me your name?

16:27.167 --> 16:28.417
It's Aarush.

16:29.208 --> 16:30.208
Aarush what?

16:32.042 --> 16:33.042
[Slightly] Singh.

16:34.667 --> 16:36.708
♪Aarush what? ♪

16:36.792 --> 16:40.583
[laughs] No, like Singh with an H.

16:41.250 --> 16:42.375
It's Indian.

16:48.875 --> 16:51.042
Well then, Aarush Singh…

16:53.792 --> 16:56.333
Let's figure out how
you're gonna make things right.

17:16.208 --> 17:18.667
Do you know why there are
three monkeys on your wall?

17:21.333 --> 17:22.333
What?

17:23.958 --> 17:26.292
Mizaru sees no evil.

17:26.375 --> 17:29.083
Kikazaru hears no evil.

17:29.167 --> 17:31.750
Iwazaru speaks no evil.

17:32.458 --> 17:37.125
They're figures in Buddhist teaching
about avoiding evil thoughts and deeds,

17:37.208 --> 17:41.458
but over the years they've come
to represent something else.

17:42.167 --> 17:43.167
Do you know what that is?

17:46.375 --> 17:47.708
No.

17:47.792 --> 17:51.208
If I did something evil,
would you report me?

17:52.875 --> 17:54.458
-I--
-If I…

17:56.667 --> 18:01.167
hurt or caused others to be hurt,

18:02.250 --> 18:05.083
would you do something to stop me
or would you look the other way?

18:07.375 --> 18:08.417
[Morrow] Careful.

18:11.333 --> 18:12.708
Everything okay?

18:14.208 --> 18:17.333
Yes. It's just, um… [breathes shakily]

18:19.000 --> 18:20.500
Is this a test?

18:22.917 --> 18:26.542
That's what being an adult is.
A constant test.

18:38.292 --> 18:39.792
I would stand up for my friends.

18:43.167 --> 18:44.250
[breathes deeply]

18:44.750 --> 18:45.750
Good.

19:28.208 --> 19:29.250
[sheep bleats]

19:32.542 --> 19:33.542
[bleats]

19:35.792 --> 19:37.042
[bleats]

19:41.375 --> 19:42.583
[squelching]

19:48.208 --> 19:50.250
[squelching]

19:58.583 --> 19:59.917
[bleats]

20:09.042 --> 20:10.042
-[bleating]
-[squelching]

20:20.375 --> 20:21.417
[bleats]

20:33.542 --> 20:34.542
[beating and squelching stop]

20:43.625 --> 20:44.833
[growling]

20:53.250 --> 20:54.375
[snarls, growling]

21:11.375 --> 21:12.458
Fascinating.

21:18.833 --> 21:21.875
Yeah, the wavy lines
are totally different now.

21:21.958 --> 21:23.292
Those are gamma waves.

21:23.375 --> 21:26.000
Problem solving, concentration.
See how they've increased?

21:26.083 --> 21:27.708
-[Tootles] Mm-hmm.
-[sheep fluttering]

21:29.625 --> 21:32.500
-This creature is highly intelligent.
-[fluttering continues]

21:37.917 --> 21:38.958
Look how it's watching us.

21:50.083 --> 21:51.583
[growls]

22:12.167 --> 22:15.458
-If one of those ever escapes--
-Glass half full, kid.

22:16.125 --> 22:18.792
Yeah? We talked about this.

22:23.208 --> 22:24.250
[ball thuds]

22:25.917 --> 22:27.000
[squeals]

22:27.083 --> 22:28.042
Hey. Hey.

22:28.792 --> 22:31.542
[growling]

22:35.667 --> 22:37.083
Mmm.

22:40.375 --> 22:41.375
Me.

22:43.500 --> 22:44.708
Yeah.

22:46.750 --> 22:47.792
Me.

22:57.208 --> 22:59.208
[birds chirping]

23:16.500 --> 23:19.500
Arthur, what is it?

23:27.333 --> 23:30.833
[huffs] Arthur, where are you taking me?

23:32.792 --> 23:35.042
I don't understand
why we had to leave the lab. What?

23:35.875 --> 23:37.083
Are you serious?

23:37.167 --> 23:40.167
Darling, can you please just
explain to me what's going on?

23:40.250 --> 23:42.542
And what are we doing here?
This isn't science.

23:42.625 --> 23:44.833
We're-We're halfway through
beta testing the hybrids,

23:44.917 --> 23:47.833
and he brings in, who knows
how many alien species. [stammers]

23:47.917 --> 23:52.458
It is a multi-world corporation.
I think we can do two things at once.

23:52.542 --> 23:56.083
Yeah, but she's not ready.
Wendy, I mean, or any of them.

23:56.167 --> 23:59.625
I mean, this-this morning proves that,
that the data isn't there.

24:00.625 --> 24:02.958
And we mapped out the steps. The--

24:03.042 --> 24:05.833
[indistinct through intercom]

24:05.917 --> 24:08.500
The control groups, the system checks.

24:09.625 --> 24:14.500
He sends them to a crash site into combat.

24:15.042 --> 24:18.500
Yes, he breaks the rules. He takes risks.

24:18.583 --> 24:20.708
Maybe that's why he's so…
[chuckles] …far ahead.

24:21.292 --> 24:23.417
Yeah, but that's not science.

24:23.500 --> 24:26.542
I mean, I don't care how rich you get.
Something is or it isn't.

24:27.875 --> 24:28.917
Quantum mechanics.

24:30.167 --> 24:32.833
-Okay, that's--
-Light is a particle and a wave.

24:32.917 --> 24:34.417
Yes, theoretically, yeah.

24:34.500 --> 24:36.917
But-But we took the minds of
children and put them in synthetic bodies.

24:37.000 --> 24:38.583
That's not theoretical.

24:39.750 --> 24:41.542
I mean, if we did this wrong,

24:41.625 --> 24:43.208
best case, we've got a bunch of AIs
running around

24:43.292 --> 24:46.000
thinking that they're human.
Worst case, we killed six kids.

24:48.417 --> 24:52.083
That is not something
we're flippant about, ever.

24:53.667 --> 24:55.208
I hear you. I just--

24:55.958 --> 24:58.167
I'm worried and I…

25:00.167 --> 25:01.500
I need you to hear that.

25:02.458 --> 25:04.875
Not as a scientist, but as your husband.

25:04.958 --> 25:07.000
I do, I do.

25:10.500 --> 25:14.333
Okay, then we need to stick to
the protocols, reevaluate the risks.

25:15.000 --> 25:17.917
I think we need to think about
what's gonna happen

25:18.000 --> 25:19.958
if this whole thing goes to hell.

25:22.083 --> 25:23.208
Listen…

25:26.458 --> 25:28.542
I'll make dinner tonight, okay?

25:29.458 --> 25:30.500
Love dumplings.

25:32.417 --> 25:33.875
We'll sit and talk this through.

25:38.500 --> 25:39.583
We'll figure it out.

25:41.250 --> 25:44.750
[humming]

25:45.250 --> 25:46.792
Hey, sweetheart.

25:47.625 --> 25:48.958
I have good news.

25:49.833 --> 25:51.125
Such good news.

25:51.875 --> 25:52.875
I'm pregnant.

25:55.292 --> 25:56.458
What?

25:56.542 --> 25:57.917
I'm gonna have a baby.

25:58.000 --> 26:00.917
No, that-- that's-- that's impossible.
You're a machine.

26:01.500 --> 26:04.333
I'm not a machine. Why would you say that?

26:05.375 --> 26:07.792
No, of course not, baby.

26:07.875 --> 26:09.417
[Dame Sylvia] He didn't mean that.

26:12.000 --> 26:15.500
You're just too young to have a child.

26:18.792 --> 26:19.875
Well, it's true.

26:20.375 --> 26:22.250
I woke up this morning and I just knew.

26:23.125 --> 26:25.333
It's not all those horrible things
he was saying about me.

26:26.333 --> 26:29.583
What's inside of me, controlling me.

26:30.250 --> 26:31.708
Metal and silicone.

26:33.375 --> 26:34.750
It's a baby.

26:37.250 --> 26:41.625
Wow, that's… [chuckles] …big news.

26:45.208 --> 26:47.958
Why don't we go to my office
and we can talk.

26:50.000 --> 26:51.708
How have you been feeling?

26:51.792 --> 26:55.000
Pretty tired.
This being pregnant thing is hard work.

27:06.125 --> 27:07.708
[Dame Sylvia] We haven't talked
in a while.

27:12.083 --> 27:15.375
And now you have this feeling.

27:16.375 --> 27:17.625
It's not just a feeling.

27:20.667 --> 27:21.833
I'm gonna have a baby.

27:24.042 --> 27:25.792
[Dame Sylvia] Well, that's interesting.

27:26.292 --> 27:31.083
Can you tell me…
Do you know how babies are made?

27:32.000 --> 27:33.125
God brings them.

27:36.917 --> 27:39.000
[Dame Sylvia] Yes, on some level.

27:41.167 --> 27:43.958
But there's also a process,

27:45.167 --> 27:48.417
a physical process
between a man and a woman.

27:49.292 --> 27:51.583
A biological process.

27:51.667 --> 27:54.583
I know about all that.
I grew up on a farm.

27:55.333 --> 27:57.250
[Dame Sylvia] Oh, well, then you know.

27:57.750 --> 28:03.083
A man and a woman,
their bodies fit together

28:03.167 --> 28:06.500
and the man's sperm fertilizes
the woman's egg.

28:07.167 --> 28:09.625
Then, the embryo grows into a baby.

28:10.167 --> 28:11.292
Except for Jesus.

28:14.000 --> 28:15.958
His mommy didn't do that, and he was born.

28:21.375 --> 28:23.500
You know what,

28:23.583 --> 28:25.583
I'm starting to realize that we didn't…

28:27.125 --> 28:28.958
[smacks lips, inhales sharply, chuckles]

28:30.417 --> 28:33.667
In all the busyness of trying to make sure

28:33.750 --> 28:36.458
that everything went right
with your transition, all of you,

28:37.625 --> 28:41.625
we never really talked about
your new bodies…

28:41.708 --> 28:43.000
[humming]

28:43.083 --> 28:46.583
…how they work, how they're different.

28:48.708 --> 28:53.500
You know it's different, right?
Your new body.

28:53.583 --> 28:55.000
[humming]

28:55.667 --> 28:57.042
It's big.

28:57.125 --> 29:02.292
It is, but it's not just the size.
I'm talking about on the inside.

29:03.583 --> 29:06.375
Let me show you a diagram.

29:08.208 --> 29:09.333
I don't want to look at that.

29:14.833 --> 29:16.708
-Here.
-I said no.

29:20.958 --> 29:21.958
Okay.

29:34.375 --> 29:38.083
It's a girl, I think. Clarissa.

29:39.167 --> 29:40.583
-Nibs.
-[Nibs] That's her name.

29:41.792 --> 29:43.167
What she wants to be called.

29:45.750 --> 29:48.083
What happened at the towers?

29:50.125 --> 29:52.542
[growls]

29:53.458 --> 29:54.792
I don't wanna talk about that.

29:56.792 --> 29:59.333
[Dame Sylvia] Curly said you
were really brave.

30:02.042 --> 30:03.542
I said I don't want to talk about it.

30:08.417 --> 30:11.375
Those creatures can't hurt you,
you know that, right?

30:12.000 --> 30:14.125
-They're all locked up.
-I said stop talking.

30:14.833 --> 30:18.667
[gasps, breathing heavily]

30:19.917 --> 30:23.083
All right, sweetheart. I'm sorry.

30:29.375 --> 30:31.250
Do you want to have some tea?

30:33.875 --> 30:36.375
I was thinking about having
a… [stammers] …cup of tea.

30:36.458 --> 30:38.458
[breathing heavily]

30:47.042 --> 30:48.125
[whispering] I'm sorry.

31:00.208 --> 31:05.667
I got those, uh, new flavor strips
that you asked for,

31:06.958 --> 31:08.917
the chamomile and mint.

31:09.750 --> 31:10.750
I like apple cinnamon.

31:12.000 --> 31:16.917
Oh, apple cinnamon, yeah. Okay, yeah,
I have that too. [breathing heavily]

31:17.000 --> 31:20.208
If it's a boy,
what do you think about Trevor?

31:21.708 --> 31:26.792
Trevor, that's… Good name.
[chuckles] Strong.

31:32.167 --> 31:36.000
Oh, sweetheart,
I… [chuckles] …I didn't realize

31:36.083 --> 31:37.958
I was meant to be meeting Mr. Kavalier.

31:38.042 --> 31:39.750
These gentlemen will help you

31:39.833 --> 31:41.208
-back to your room, okay?
-Hmm. Okay.

31:42.875 --> 31:44.250
Can I keep him?

31:45.542 --> 31:46.583
[chuckling] Of course.

31:49.958 --> 31:52.417
And, listen, we'll talk again soon, okay?

31:54.500 --> 31:56.083
-I've missed our talks.
-Okay.

32:01.875 --> 32:04.333
[breathes shakily]

32:04.417 --> 32:05.667
Get her back to her room.

32:05.750 --> 32:07.083
Secure the door.

32:07.167 --> 32:09.375
Tell your supervisor
we have a level three event.

32:09.458 --> 32:10.458
He'll know what to do.

32:11.125 --> 32:15.750
-Yes, ma'am.
-[breathing heavily]

32:26.042 --> 32:27.333
[Hermit] You wanted to see me?

32:29.000 --> 32:31.833
I thought we might discuss
your future with this company.

32:34.000 --> 32:39.333
My unit, sir, specifically Rashidi,
Siberian, are they okay?

32:39.917 --> 32:41.750
That information is confidential.

32:42.667 --> 32:45.875
To be completely frank,
so is the information about Wendy.

32:46.583 --> 32:49.583
Marcy. My sister's name is Marcy.

32:52.625 --> 32:54.333
So you believe she's your sister?

32:55.375 --> 33:01.458
She has her memories, her sense of humor.
Doesn't that make her Marcy?

33:02.458 --> 33:04.917
Actually, that's something
we'd like to know too.

33:05.833 --> 33:07.167
From you.

33:08.000 --> 33:09.250
From me?

33:09.333 --> 33:12.042
We'd like confirmation
from someone who knew her well

33:12.125 --> 33:15.417
that the transition was a success.

33:16.208 --> 33:17.833
She thinks she's my sister.

33:18.917 --> 33:19.917
What do you think?

33:23.292 --> 33:25.333
I think this conversation's over.

33:26.125 --> 33:27.458
It's a condition of your employment

33:27.542 --> 33:29.667
that you answer
all questions put to you by a superior.

33:29.750 --> 33:30.958
Well, then, I quit.

33:33.958 --> 33:34.958
Of course.

33:37.208 --> 33:41.167
As this is a private island, we'll have to
get you on the first shuttle back home.

33:41.250 --> 33:43.083
Confidentiality, you understand.

33:44.083 --> 33:46.917
I should tell you that
if you're no longer employed by us,

33:47.000 --> 33:49.750
then all contact with the unit
you call your sister

33:49.833 --> 33:51.417
-will be terminated.
-No. [grunts] No.

33:51.500 --> 33:55.208
Also, as your body keeps reminding you,
you have a new lung.

33:55.292 --> 33:59.250
Whilst it comes courtesy of
the Prodigy Corporation, it isn't free.

33:59.333 --> 34:01.417
You could work off the price here,

34:01.500 --> 34:05.625
assessing Wendy, redeploy
and pay it off with a lifetime contract,

34:05.708 --> 34:09.167
or simply go home, and we'll bill you.

34:22.958 --> 34:26.667
Did you really download
my sister's consciousness into that body?

34:29.167 --> 34:30.583
Is it her?

34:30.667 --> 34:32.667
Those are two different questions.

34:33.792 --> 34:38.375
One is practical and the other,
well, that's the real existential crux,

34:39.500 --> 34:43.792
and, I should add, the difference between
a trillion-dollar business

34:43.875 --> 34:45.750
and a blanket with sleeves.

34:46.750 --> 34:47.958
A what?

34:48.542 --> 34:49.958
An invention that no one wanted.

34:52.000 --> 34:55.542
So, are you still an employee of
the Prodigy Corporation,

34:55.625 --> 34:57.875
or shall I have security
remove you from the island?

34:58.458 --> 34:59.458
No, no.

35:02.750 --> 35:04.208
[breathes heavily] I'm your man.

35:06.292 --> 35:07.292
Excellent.

35:09.000 --> 35:10.458
And when can I take her home?

35:14.708 --> 35:17.875
Let me be clear about something,
Prodigy Medic Hermit.

35:18.958 --> 35:24.125
The unit you call your sister is
the property of the Prodigy Corporation.

35:24.208 --> 35:26.500
She is a prototype for
a transhuman product

35:26.583 --> 35:30.833
which, once refined,
will create human immortality.

35:30.917 --> 35:32.708
This makes her the next evolutionary step

35:32.792 --> 35:36.167
between our animal past
and our transhuman future.

35:38.750 --> 35:42.542
There's no universe in which
you get to take her home.

35:43.500 --> 35:44.667
Are we clear?

35:44.750 --> 35:47.292
-She is still a human being.
-No.

35:48.208 --> 35:49.208
She's not.

35:51.292 --> 35:52.292
Not anymore.

36:01.958 --> 36:04.333
[Rashidi] Smell that? What is that?

36:04.417 --> 36:08.750
I don't know. It smells like a mix of
bad luck and loser.

36:09.375 --> 36:11.500
[Hermit] How the hell?

36:15.292 --> 36:16.292
[chuckles]

36:17.208 --> 36:19.375
[Hermit] So, what, you… you work here now?

36:20.500 --> 36:22.125
[Rashidi through screen]
Yeah. Whole squad.

36:22.208 --> 36:23.417
[Siberian] Well, what's left of us.

36:35.625 --> 36:36.708
[Boy Kavalier] Weird day.

36:40.375 --> 36:41.750
I'm supposed to meet with Sylvia.

36:43.792 --> 36:44.875
[Boy Kavalier] Have a seat.

37:01.333 --> 37:02.375
Pirates or Indians?

37:05.208 --> 37:06.208
Um…

37:07.042 --> 37:08.292
When you fought the alien,

37:09.167 --> 37:11.833
was it more like fighting pirates
or Indians?

37:14.292 --> 37:15.458
[chuckling] What?

37:15.542 --> 37:17.250
It's Peter Pan.

37:18.542 --> 37:20.417
Wait, the crocodile.

37:21.000 --> 37:27.500
You fought the crocodile,
and now you can hear the clock, you know?

37:27.583 --> 37:29.625
Tick, tick, tick, tick, tick, tick.

37:30.750 --> 37:32.083
It was hurting Joe.

37:32.167 --> 37:34.375
Oh, right, the brother.

37:34.458 --> 37:36.583
What do you think they're saying?
The aliens.

37:37.792 --> 37:39.250
You shouldn't have cut them open.

37:39.333 --> 37:41.000
[stammers] They… They said that?

37:41.542 --> 37:44.875
No, I… I don't know.

37:48.208 --> 37:50.333
How would you feel if they cut you open?

37:50.417 --> 37:53.583
Or, uh, stuck to my face or drank my blood

37:53.667 --> 37:55.667
or popped out my eyeball
and burrowed into my brain.

37:55.750 --> 37:58.875
I mean, they would if they could,
you know, so why not just cut them open?

38:01.542 --> 38:04.917
Should we let him stay, you think?
The brother?

38:05.000 --> 38:08.083
Give him access to all this? You?

38:09.250 --> 38:11.708
Please. He's nice.

38:12.458 --> 38:17.917
[clicks tongue, stammers] It's nice
if you like brothers, which I don't.

38:19.500 --> 38:24.417
Or fathers or, ugh, mothers, you know.
[grunts] Always telling us what to do.

38:25.542 --> 38:29.042
Why do you think she wanted them?
Yutani, the aliens.

38:31.833 --> 38:33.667
I think we should send them back.

38:33.750 --> 38:35.042
[Boy Kavalier] Because they hurt you?

38:35.542 --> 38:36.833
Because it's wrong keeping them.

38:37.500 --> 38:40.958
Uh… No. I'll tell you who
we should send back. Your brother.

38:41.042 --> 38:42.042
[clicks tongue]

38:43.958 --> 38:45.833
-[Wendy] Please don't.
-[Boy Kavalier] None of the others

38:45.917 --> 38:47.708
get to have brothers.
What makes you so special?

38:47.792 --> 38:49.250
I'm not special. I just…

38:55.208 --> 38:57.292
-Please.
-Oh, come on. Come on. [scoffs]

38:58.042 --> 39:00.042
What kind of intellectual argument
is "please"?

39:00.125 --> 39:02.792
No, I'm sorry. We should send him back
on the next plane home.

39:02.875 --> 39:04.375
I mean, if you think about it…

39:04.458 --> 39:07.250
[inhales deeply] …he didn't even come
visit you when you were sick.

39:11.417 --> 39:14.750
Joe was working. He was far away.

39:14.833 --> 39:17.375
[imitating Hermit] Oh, my God.
It's too far. It's too hard.

39:17.458 --> 39:20.875
All I know is if I was your brother,
I'd find a way to be there.

39:28.750 --> 39:30.083
I tell you what.

39:31.750 --> 39:35.125
If you help me figure out
what the aliens are saying,

39:36.250 --> 39:39.750
I'll let you keep him. Okay?
But the minute he becomes a distraction--

39:39.833 --> 39:42.208
He won't. I promise.

39:45.708 --> 39:46.750
Pinky promise.

40:00.500 --> 40:01.833
[smacks lips] Okay.

40:04.125 --> 40:05.292
Away with you.

40:16.542 --> 40:21.083
[growling]

40:21.583 --> 40:23.583
[electronic screeching]

40:29.250 --> 40:32.042
[growling]

40:36.375 --> 40:37.542
You know what?

40:37.625 --> 40:38.625
Hmm?

40:41.208 --> 40:42.375
Can I change my name?

40:45.583 --> 40:47.333
-You want to talk about this now?
-Um…

40:48.542 --> 40:51.250
I'm a grown man. A-A scientist.

40:51.333 --> 40:54.375
I-I want a real name.

40:56.833 --> 40:58.458
Our leader named you when you were born.

40:58.542 --> 41:04.958
I know, but that's like a--
It's like a code name like Sport or Chief.

41:05.042 --> 41:08.708
It's-It's not what you call a man.
You know, a serious man.

41:10.833 --> 41:12.208
What would you like to be called?

41:12.292 --> 41:14.333
Well, I was thinking about… [stammers]
…scientists.

41:14.417 --> 41:19.333
You know, like famous thinkers.
And I was thinking Isaac.

41:20.083 --> 41:21.500
-After--
-Isaac Newton.

41:26.000 --> 41:27.708
Yes, I think that's all right.

41:38.625 --> 41:39.625
[sighs, sniffles]

41:43.500 --> 41:44.792
-Oh, Hey.
-Hey.

41:44.875 --> 41:45.875
I was just, uh…

41:47.500 --> 41:50.833
I'm doing proper science now.
Um, Kirsh really relies on me.

41:51.625 --> 41:54.500
Kirsh. Wow. Well, that's awesome.

41:54.583 --> 41:56.250
-Yeah. And I've got a new name.
-Uh-huh.

41:56.333 --> 41:58.625
Isaac, after the old-time scientist.

41:58.708 --> 42:01.292
[chuckles] Where'd you get that?

42:01.375 --> 42:02.708
Gave it to myself.

42:02.792 --> 42:04.042
Wow, we can do that?

42:05.042 --> 42:08.708
I don't know about "can," but I did.
Anyway, Kirsh said it was okay, so…

42:10.583 --> 42:12.292
I"m-I'm helping too, you know. Um…

42:12.792 --> 42:16.125
Arthur said that
he needs something from the lab.

42:17.375 --> 42:21.083
Oh. Cool. What?

42:22.875 --> 42:24.042
Huh?

42:24.917 --> 42:26.667
Yeah, what does Arthur need from the lab?

42:27.833 --> 42:30.375
Oh. Oh, it was, um…

42:31.333 --> 42:32.333
[stutters]

42:35.667 --> 42:38.125
[inhales deeply] Okay, well,
we're gonna go play some games.

42:39.333 --> 42:44.208
So come.
And then you'll remember, probably.

42:46.417 --> 42:51.333
Actually, I'm just gonna take a walk.
Just clear my head.

42:51.958 --> 42:53.125
You sure?

42:53.208 --> 42:57.833
Yeah, I'm just, uh, you know…
I'll see you later. Bye.

42:59.125 --> 43:00.125
[Isaac] Cool.

43:16.542 --> 43:17.542
[typing]

43:20.625 --> 43:23.167
[Slightly] Hey, uh, hello?

43:24.292 --> 43:25.875
It's me.

43:27.375 --> 43:30.042
I'm not sure how this works, but, um…

43:31.458 --> 43:32.625
can you hear me?

43:34.083 --> 43:35.708
-[Morrow] I can hear you.
-Oh.

43:36.250 --> 43:38.167
Okay, well, great.

43:38.250 --> 43:40.208
I-I checked it out, you know, the lab.

43:41.375 --> 43:44.083
And there's just no way, you know.

43:44.167 --> 43:46.958
You're a very clever boy.
You'll figure it out.

43:47.042 --> 43:52.708
[Slightly] Oh, no, no. W-What I'm trying
to say is that… I can't do it.

43:53.542 --> 43:55.583
Can't or won't?

44:00.375 --> 44:01.500
They're my friends.

44:02.167 --> 44:03.542
[sighs] So won't.

44:04.250 --> 44:05.250
[Slightly] I'm sorry, Kumi.

44:06.208 --> 44:09.125
[sighs] Aarush,
I'm afraid "sorry" doesn't work.

44:10.250 --> 44:11.750
You know, it's funny you called.

44:11.833 --> 44:13.333
I was just talking about you.

44:14.583 --> 44:15.583
With your mother.

44:18.500 --> 44:19.500
What?

44:19.583 --> 44:22.708
She thought you were dead.
Isn't that awful?

44:23.875 --> 44:25.958
-Mummy?
-[Morrow] Oh, she can't hear you.

44:26.042 --> 44:27.792
This is our private line.

44:27.875 --> 44:29.625
But you know what she told me?

44:29.708 --> 44:33.750
Your brothers will be home soon.
I can't wait to meet them.

44:34.333 --> 44:35.917
No, no, no, no. Please, please, please.

44:36.000 --> 44:40.167
[chuckling] Oh. Now it's "please"?

44:40.250 --> 44:42.792
You know, Aarush,
I really wanted us to be friends.

44:42.875 --> 44:45.125
[Slightly stutters] We are, right?
We are friends.

44:45.667 --> 44:48.000
I hope so,
but friends help each other, right?

44:53.292 --> 44:54.292
I'll help.

44:55.500 --> 44:58.083
Well, that's great. What a relief.

44:58.167 --> 44:59.167
He's gonna help.

44:59.250 --> 45:01.750
[Slightly] I just don't know how,
you know? Like, th-there's just one of me

45:01.833 --> 45:04.042
and there's so many guards
and they have guns and--

45:04.125 --> 45:05.708
[Morrow] I'll tell you
what you're gonna do.

45:06.542 --> 45:10.083
Instead of sneaking out an egg,
you're gonna sneak in a person.

45:12.792 --> 45:13.792
[Slightly] A person?

45:14.292 --> 45:16.917
Into the lab. Anyone you like.

45:17.500 --> 45:20.083
Take them to see the eggs.
Get them good and close.

45:21.792 --> 45:23.083
Why?

45:23.167 --> 45:25.542
Well, the egg is gonna hatch
and something's gonna come out.

45:25.625 --> 45:28.667
Don't worry. It doesn't want you.

45:28.750 --> 45:32.042
Something's gonna come out
and attach itself to the person's face.

45:32.125 --> 45:34.792
And he or she is gonna fall down,

45:34.875 --> 45:39.208
and you need to find
some place quiet to keep them.

45:39.292 --> 45:41.167
Not long, just a day or two.

45:42.417 --> 45:45.583
And the thing on the person's face
is gonna fall off.

45:46.667 --> 45:47.875
And they're gonna wake up.

45:52.125 --> 45:54.125
And that-that-that's
when I bring them to you?

45:54.208 --> 45:55.500
[Morrow] Exactly.

45:56.083 --> 45:58.458
[Morrow through screen]
But you'll have to be quick, okay?

45:59.542 --> 46:00.958
[Slightly stammering] Okay, but how do I--

46:01.042 --> 46:02.917
[Morrow] Don't worry, I'll be close.

46:03.583 --> 46:06.292
You call me and I'll meet you.

46:10.750 --> 46:12.333
What happens to the person I choose?

46:12.417 --> 46:16.333
Well, they'll have a bad couple of days,
for sure,

46:16.417 --> 46:20.542
but I'll make sure they're comfortable.
Understand?

46:22.667 --> 46:26.417
No. I-I don't understand,
like, how… how do I… how do I choose?

46:26.500 --> 46:30.208
Oh, looks like
your mom wants to say something.

46:31.208 --> 46:33.125
Did you want to say something to Aarush?

46:36.417 --> 46:37.417
[Slightly's mother] Aarush.

46:38.583 --> 46:39.583
Mummy?

46:40.500 --> 46:41.833
We love you, beta.

46:41.917 --> 46:45.417
[crying] You know, every day your brothers
and me talk about you.

46:45.500 --> 46:46.625
-Aarush--
-[Morrow shushes]

46:48.583 --> 46:49.583
Mummy?

46:50.375 --> 46:51.917
[Morrow] Isn't that sweet?

46:52.958 --> 46:55.208
But I've gotta go,
you know what you've gotta do.

46:55.958 --> 47:00.750
Let's say you have 24 hours to choose
someone and get them in front of the eggs.

47:01.458 --> 47:04.458
-[breathing heavily, stuttering] Twenty--
-[Morrow] A day, I'm saying.

47:04.542 --> 47:06.333
-A day. Okay.
-[Morrow] This time tomorrow.

47:09.292 --> 47:12.500
Hey, remember before?

47:12.583 --> 47:14.417
When we talked and you said all that stuff

47:14.500 --> 47:17.833
about how you'd do anything you had to
to protect your parents?

47:20.125 --> 47:23.708
This is that. Are we clear?

47:25.042 --> 47:29.042
[whispers] Yes, we're clear. Okay.

47:31.583 --> 47:34.208
Okay. Great.

47:34.833 --> 47:38.417
You're doing great. Now, go get 'em.

47:38.500 --> 47:40.500
[breathing shakily]

47:45.333 --> 47:46.375
[beeping, ticking]

48:22.500 --> 48:23.500
[birds chirping]

48:36.125 --> 48:38.333
-[Smee] No, no, no. No.
-[Wendy] No, not that one.

48:38.417 --> 48:40.208
-No. Try this one.
-I don't get to choose?

48:40.292 --> 48:41.500
[whispers] Just try it.

48:41.583 --> 48:42.917
It's good. It's good. You'll like it.

48:47.875 --> 48:49.667
Tastes like motor oil. Jesus.

48:49.750 --> 48:52.708
[Wendy] But it doesn't really have
a flavor. It's like a sequence to it.

48:52.792 --> 48:54.250
[Isaac] It's a line of code, actually.

48:54.333 --> 48:57.833
It's like a subroutine that triggers
a synthetic memory of a flavor.

48:57.917 --> 48:59.583
-[snores]
-[Isaac] Like script.

49:00.167 --> 49:01.167
Smarty-pants.

49:01.250 --> 49:02.292
Well, I've been reading a lot.

49:02.375 --> 49:06.000
You know, Arthur has these computer chips,
like, um… like a library.

49:06.083 --> 49:07.250
You just upload them

49:07.333 --> 49:09.792
and suddenly all the information
from the book is suddenly in your head.

49:09.875 --> 49:12.542
-Wait, we can do that?
-[Isaac] Mm-hmm. Anything you want.

49:12.625 --> 49:16.625
I'm learning zoology and advanced biology,
so I can help Kirsh out in the lab.

49:18.208 --> 49:19.708
Can I ask you a question?

49:21.333 --> 49:22.417
Of course.

49:23.917 --> 49:25.083
What's the Five?

49:26.333 --> 49:28.417
The boss,
he keeps talking about "the Five."

49:28.500 --> 49:33.125
[Hermit] Uh, well, the old planet had
something called governments,

49:33.208 --> 49:37.167
where people used to… They would vote for
who they wanted making decisions, right?

49:38.917 --> 49:39.958
It didn't work.

49:40.667 --> 49:43.667
So these five corporations, they rose up

49:43.750 --> 49:46.833
and they apparently…
they fixed all the problems.

49:46.917 --> 49:49.167
Now they work together to run things.

49:49.250 --> 49:51.375
Prodigy, Weyland-Yutani…

49:51.458 --> 49:53.792
-[Hermit] Threshold, Dynamic, Lynch.
-And Lynch. Yeah.

49:53.875 --> 49:56.583
And the ship that crashed,
it was Yutani's, and we took it.

49:56.667 --> 49:58.292
-And they're mad.
-[inhales deeply]

49:58.375 --> 50:00.375
-Mmm.
-So, they're probably asking the Five

50:00.458 --> 50:02.625
to step in and get their stuff back.

50:03.958 --> 50:04.958
Their creatures?

50:05.958 --> 50:06.958
Yeah.

50:07.042 --> 50:08.375
[Isaac] They're life-forms.

50:08.458 --> 50:10.375
We've learned so much from them already.

50:10.458 --> 50:12.792
Well, if you love them so much,
why don't you just marry them?

50:12.875 --> 50:14.375
-[mocking]
-[laughs]

50:14.458 --> 50:19.042
[laughs] Hey, hey. We're eating
ice cream and we're talking.

50:20.708 --> 50:22.125
How come he's here?

50:25.625 --> 50:26.750
He's my brother.

50:27.833 --> 50:29.375
I've got brothers too.

50:29.458 --> 50:31.625
-We've all got brothers and mothers.
-Slightly.

50:31.708 --> 50:32.958
And if they're not here,

50:33.042 --> 50:35.542
-he shouldn't be either.
-[Wendy] Hey, you're being rude.

50:37.042 --> 50:38.625
You're not the boss of us.

50:40.167 --> 50:41.167
I'm the oldest.

50:42.417 --> 50:46.750
No. Curly's what, like 12?

50:46.833 --> 50:48.042
And Isaac too.

50:48.125 --> 50:49.167
[Hermit] I agree.

50:50.125 --> 50:52.417
Your families should be here, all of them.

50:54.083 --> 50:57.083
Instead of having funerals,
they should have had parties.

50:58.083 --> 51:02.625
You were dying and now look at you.

51:05.333 --> 51:08.958
Yeah, we should… we should ask
to see our families.

51:09.042 --> 51:11.167
No. Demand.

51:11.250 --> 51:12.750
Yeah. See my mom.

51:12.833 --> 51:14.792
No. They'll get mad
and might send us away.

51:14.875 --> 51:16.708
-And I like it here.
-Me too. I mean,

51:16.792 --> 51:19.458
we get respect here,
and they've made us into something.

51:19.542 --> 51:20.542
[Curly] Yeah.

51:27.833 --> 51:28.917
I'm gonna go.

51:30.667 --> 51:33.792
The boy genius said to go to the lab,
so I'm gonna go.

51:34.625 --> 51:36.000
You don't have to.

51:36.792 --> 51:38.042
I want to.

51:57.542 --> 51:59.417
[chirring]

52:11.500 --> 52:14.083
-[chirring continues]
-[Wendy chirrs]

52:51.875 --> 52:52.875
[chirring continues]

52:58.125 --> 53:01.542
[Wendy chirring]

53:19.083 --> 53:21.083
[humming]

53:28.208 --> 53:30.208
[vocalizing]

53:50.250 --> 53:51.250
[vocalizing stops]

54:28.833 --> 54:30.958
[screeching]

55:06.333 --> 55:07.417
[screeching continues]

55:13.042 --> 55:14.875
[Wendy breathing heavily]

55:26.000 --> 55:27.000
[screeches]

55:39.208 --> 55:41.042
["Ocean Size" playing]
